# The preset policy in kgsm-base decides whether this daemon runs on a node; applying it is symlink
# manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. Starting it is the
# node's post-transaction hook's job, which is where a running system is available.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who disabled this unit keeps it disabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly undo that.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-watchdog.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-watchdog needs no configuration: /etc/kgsm-watchdog/kgsm-watchdog.env holds overrides only,
  and every one of them has a working default.

  It owns kgsm.slice and supervises native instances. Autostart and crash-restart are its desired
  state, held per instance — `kgsm autostart enable <instance>` is what makes one come up on boot.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-watchdog/kgsm-watchdog.env, merge it — new settings are
  declared there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}
